there is a super interesting article in  the new york times about  tucker carlson and journalists and  anonymous sources that i want to look at  with you today  and what's most interesting there's a  few things that are interesting about  this article one are the things we learn  about tucker carlson specifically  but also what we learn about this  relationship in terms of  trading uh for access that exists  in general in politics but also in the  political media space so let's jump  right into it the article  in the new york times called tucker  carlson calls journalists  animals he's also their best source  and the article by ben smith starts  last month i texted tucker carlson to  ask him a question that was on my mind  did you get vaccinated when was the last  time you had sex with your wife  and in what position he replied we can  trade  intimate details then we argued back and  forth about vaccines  and he ended the conversation with a  friendly invitation to return to his  show  always a good time one question you may  be asking if you're a new york times  reader is  why are you exchanging texts with tucker  carlson the fox news host who recently  described the media at large  as cringing animals who are not worthy  of respect and if you're a tucker  carlson viewer you may also be asking  how can the guy who tells you every  night that the media is lying  be texting with the enemy the answer  is one of washington's open secrets mr  carlson a proud traitor  to the elite political class spends his  time when he's not denouncing the  liberal media  trading gossip with them he's the go-to  guy for sometimes unflattering stories  about donald j  trump and for coverage of the internal  politics of fox news  not to mention stories about carlson  himself i won't talk here about any  off-the-record conversations i may have  had with him  but 16 other journalists none from the  times  told me on background that he has been  as three of them put it a great  source in trump's washington tucker  carlson  is a primary super secret source the  media writer and trump chronicler  michael wolff writes in his forthcoming  collection of essays  he explained i know this because i know  what he has told me  and i can track his exquisite too good  not to be true gossip  through unsourced reports and as it  often emerges into the accepted wisdom  so takeaway number one  is we knew it but this is confirmation  tucker carlson plays a character on his  program  um in the midst of all the anti-vaccine  stuff he's almost certainly been  vaccinated as as has his family i'm sure  um in the midst of all of his attacks on  the media  and his sort of cow towing to this  populist stuff that he does  uh he is a sort of regular  uh elite insider that communicates with  all these very same people  shouldn't surprise us we already  suspected that article goes on to say mr  carlson was particularly well positioned  to be a source about the trump  administration  his fox platform where in may he had a  nightly average of three million viewers  made him someone who mattered to trump a  close follower of television ratings he  has a former reporter's eye for detail  and anecdote  and his observations can be detected in  the lurid tales of trump's chaotic court  and fox's own tumultuous internal  politics  a coming book by the reporter michael  bender  includes a moment in which carlson sends  trump's calls to voicemail  after the first presidential debate last  fall when he was  criticized for repeatedly interrupting  joe biden when trump  finally reaches carlson the book  describes verbatim an exchange between  the two men that cast mr carlson in a  flattering light  everyone says i did a good job trump  tells carlson i don't know who told you  that was good mr carlson says  it was not good mr bender declined to  comment on the sourcing  that allowed him to so precisely  reconstruct a conversation between the  two men of course  the belief would be well the source is  tucker himself  the article continues brian stelter the  host of the cnn show reliable sources  told me quote you can see tucker's  fingerprints all over the hardcover  edition of his 2022 book  hoax which excoriates fox for amplifying  trump's falsehoods  he said he couldn't stomach talking to  carlson who has grown ever more  hard-line  for the updated paperback version that  was just released  carlson was born to a world of insiders  and story shapers and makes no secret of  it  his father was a reporter in la and san  diego before reagan appointed him  director of voice of  america and the son grew up with a  generation of elite washington  journalists  in 2018 tucker said i've always lived  around people who were wielding  authority  around the ruling class  a former new york observer media writer  sridhar papu  recalled to me that when he first  traveled to washington to cover the  white house correspondents association  dinner in the 2000s it was carlson who  asked him  do you have an invitation to tammy's  referring to the annual brunch  for media insiders co-hosted by tammy  haddad  the well-connected former msnbc producer  so here you start to get the sense  that not only is tucker not the guy  waging war on the so-called elite media  he's actually a guy trading on his  membership  in that very group in his private life  surprising  not at all not at all couple other  interesting things here  carlson said he turned against his  fellow elites after the 2008 financial  crisis  his political shift also transformed his  long journeyman's career as a writer  magazine writer and msnbc conservative  and make him fox's leading tribune  of the pro-trump masses but his decades  of washington relationships have  produced a tiresome conversation among  carlson's old friends  about what he really stands for whether  he's really a racist or whether he  cynically plays one on tv who knows and  what does it matter anyway  that's the same analysis of trump when  we've said  oh it sort of seems like trump plays coy  with anti-semitic stuff but his daughter  converted to judaism his grandkids are  jewish his son-in-law etc etc  what you have to remember is that it  doesn't actually matter in the end  if trump uh placates and coalesces that  very group  it doesn't really matter what's in his  head and that is amplified  with tucker carlson as well and the  article points out as we have  carlson's recent fixations include  suggesting the january 6th capital  insurrection was  a provocation staged by the fbi and that  making children wear masks is abuse  do we know whether that is really  tucker's view or not no we don't i  suspect it's  not uh but that is uh  something that will remain a question so  the takeaways are  tucker's just like all of these other  people there's really no difference he  is the very same type of elite that he  decries  and unfortunately and i don't want to  make this a bigger thing about populism  and grifting in general  but there is a lot of populist rhetoric  that is rhetoric alone and it is not  actually the beliefs of the individuals  behind it and that can apply on the left  and the right  on the right a lot of the populist  rhetoric is a cover for just a sort of  like  elite typical uh dude like tucker  on the left sometimes the populist  rhetoric is cover  for an authoritarian tendency which  we've also talked about before  last thing you know you might remember  donald trump's  discussions of anonymous sources  anonymous sources shouldn't be allowed  trump even seemingly misunderstanding  that an anonymous source  isn't anonymous to the journalist it's  typically that the journalist is just  saying  i won't identify you it's very  interesting that such  a notable figure on the right is  such a prominent anonymous source when  he's supposedly part of a movement that  decries the status quo of the media  which as fomented by donald trump during  his presidency  includes the criticism of the use of  anonymous sources so  nothing massively surprising in the  article it confirms a lot of things we  suspected  uh but very interesting in that our  suspicions seem to be  have been very much accurate about  tucker carlson  if you ever feel like you just don't  have enough time to read all the books  you want to read  you have to check out one of my favorite  apps called blinkist blinkist takes  thousands of popular nonfiction books  and distills each one down into an  e-book  or audiobook that you can get through in  just 15 minutes where you're getting all  the key takeaways from the book  just imagine how you'll be able to  expand your horizons and knowledge  by being able to soak up all of the  important insights from 10 different  books  in an afternoon obviously it's perfect  for exposing yourself to a new book  you otherwise wouldn't have time for or  you can use it to revisit a book you've  already read  or use it to preview a book before you  buy the full version and read it  i recently read a brief history of time  of course by the great stephen hawking  this is a book that i have been aware of  for so  long and other things got in the way and  it was  fantastic to check it out on blinkist  blinkist has books on politics  philosophy science they have 27  different non-fiction categories and a  subscription is only about eight bucks a  month  and you get the entire library but  you can try it totally free and get 25  off a subscription  when you go to blinkist.com pacman  i've put the link right underneath this  video
 

